<SolutionPage
  title="PSX Screener, Scanner & Tested Strategies"
  h1="Screen the PSX, then test the idea"
  description="Screen every listed Pakistani company, run tested strategies against real history, and see the model fair value four ways. Backtests with no lookahead. Research, not advice."
  path="/solutions/psx-screener-and-strategies/"
  kicker="For active investors"
  icon="strategies"
  lead="A screener is only useful if what it surfaces has been tested. Both halves live on the same desk."
  problem="Screening the PSX is easy. Knowing whether a screen has ever worked is the hard part, and it is where most tools stop — they hand you a filtered list and leave the question of whether that filter has any predictive value entirely to you. A rule that looks obvious on today's chart usually looks obvious because you are reading history backwards."
  appPlan="pro"
  features={[
    {
      icon: 'strategies',
      title: 'Strategies tested on real history',
      body: 'Every strategy is a versioned rule file with a published backtest. Change a rule and it becomes a new version with a fresh backtest — old results never silently apply to new rules.',
    },
    {
      icon: 'method',
      title: 'No lookahead, enforced',
      body: 'A signal at any bar may use only data available at that bar’s close. Fills use the next open or that close — never a price the signal could not have known.',
    },
    {
      icon: 'value',
      title: 'Fair value, four ways',
      body: 'Four independent valuation methods rather than one number with false precision. Where they disagree, the disagreement is the useful part.',
    },
  ]}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: 'How do I know the backtests are honest?',
      a: 'Two structural guards. Strategy files are frozen once they have produced a published backtest — a changed rule becomes a new version, so an old backtest can never be quoted for a rule it did not test. And no signal may use data from after the bar it fires on, which is the assumption that makes most published backtests worthless.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Does it place trades?',
      a: 'No. Henneth never places an order. It researches, scores and monitors; execution is entirely yours. That is a deliberate design constraint, not a missing feature.',
    },
    {
      q: 'What happens when a strategy stops working?',
      a: 'It is visible. Closed positions are reviewed and the lessons written into a log the desk reads on every cycle. Losses are expected and documented rather than quietly dropped from the record.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Is intraday supported?',
      a: 'No. The desk runs on a daily timeframe. Cycles run every thirty minutes for monitoring, but there are no intraday scalping setups — that is a rule, not a roadmap item.',
    },
  ]}
>
  <h2 class="mid-h">The record is public</h2>
  <p class="mid-p">
    Every dated call — the desk's own and those of Pakistani research houses — is timestamped and
    graded against what the price actually did afterwards. Misses included. You can read
    <a href="/research/">the scored record</a> before deciding whether any of this is worth
    your attention, which is the correct order.
  </p>
  <p class="mid-p">
    If you want to understand the mechanics the desk runs on, the <a href="/blog/">writing</a>
    covers settlement, book closure and index construction from the primary regulations — including
    where PSX's own published documents currently contradict each other.
  </p>
</SolutionPage>

